# Ruby on Rails testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' testing rules to Rails: RSpec, with the Minitest equivalent where it differs. Applies only to `*.rb` files.

## Layout and framework choice

- Match the project's framework (`spec/` with RSpec `*_spec.rb`, or `test/` with Minitest `*_test.rb`); do not introduce the other.
- Mirror `app/` under `spec/`/`test/` (`app/models/order.rb` gets `spec/models/order_spec.rb`).

## RSpec conventions

- Use `context` for a precondition and `it` for the observable behavior; no vague `it "works"`.
- Use `let`/`let!` for shared fixtures and `before` for side effects, not repeated setup per `it`.
- Prefer request specs (`spec/requests`) over controller specs for new Rails apps; use system specs (`spec/system`, Capybara) for browser flows; pick the narrowest layer that covers the change.
- Use FactoryBot (`create`/`build`, `build_stubbed` when persistence is not needed) if configured, matching existing factories.

## Minitest conventions (when the project uses it instead)

- Write `test "does something" do ... end` in `ActiveSupport::TestCase` (or `ActionDispatch::IntegrationTest` for request tests); fixtures come from `test/fixtures/*.yml` or the project's factory setup.
- Use `assert_equal expected, actual` (expected first) and `assert_predicate`/`assert_raises` over a bare `assert` on a boolean.

## Database and external boundaries

- Rely on per-example transactional rollback; do not manually clean up records unless the app has opted out (e.g. system specs across threads), then match its cleanup strategy (DatabaseCleaner, truncation).
- Stub external HTTP calls at the boundary (WebMock, VCR cassettes, or the project's tooling); no real network calls.
- Assert enqueuing with `have_enqueued_job` (RSpec) / `assert_enqueued_with` (Minitest); to exercise `perform`, drain with `perform_enqueued_jobs`.

## Determinism

- Never synchronize with `sleep`; use `perform_enqueued_jobs`, a Capybara wait matcher (`have_content`), or a polling helper with a timeout.
- Freeze time (`travel_to`/`Timecop`, matching the project) for any timestamp or time-dependent assertion.
